# The HQL type system

Three mechanisms do the work, and they are easy to confuse because all three end
with a value being used at a stronger type than it started with.

| Mechanism | Written | The question it answers |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Subtyping | `Card <: Doc` | Which types stand in for which? |
| Generics | `Graph[N, E]` | What does this type leave unknown? |
| Refinement | `card.header.database : PostgresConfig` | What is known about *this value*? |

Subtyping relates whole type domains. Generics abstract over them. Refinement
concerns evidence about one value, whose representation never changes. They meet
at exactly one place — a **bound**, `T <: Doc`, where a generic unknown is
restricted by the subtype relation — and that single meeting point is why one
relation symbol is enough for the whole language.

## `<:` narrows

`Card <: Doc` means that `Card` is a **specialization** of `Doc`: every card
satisfies the contract of a document, while adding structure or stronger
constraints of its own. The relation reads from the specific toward the general,
and a value of the subtype is usable wherever the supertype is expected.

Nothing is extended or mutated by writing it. `Doc` is unchanged and unaware;
declaring `Card <: Doc` makes a claim about `Card` alone. That is why narrowing
composes freely — `Card <: Doc` and `Person <: Card` give `Person <: Doc`
without either declaration being consulted about the other.

### A type may narrow several at once

```hql
type X <: {A, B, C}
```

The braces are the ordinary set literal, and the reading is the obvious one: `X`
is a subtype of every type in this set. It is what a type system with an
intersection operator would write `A & B & C`, obtained without spending `&` on
it. In supertype position the grammar knows a set of types is a supertype set,
which is what keeps `{A, B}` and `{a: A, b: B}` apart there as everywhere else.

The supertype set is normalized, so an ancestor reachable twice is present once:
`{Card, Doc}` where `Card <: Doc` already holds says no more than `{Card}`, and
the redundancy is worth a warning rather than an error.

### What `<:` is not

`<:` states what a value **is**. Three other relations end with a value being
used at a stronger type, and none of them is subtyping.

**Capability is not ancestry.** `Graph <: Renderable[Html]` is tempting and
wrong in the same way for every renderable type: it declares a parent whose
implementation does not exist anywhere in the type graph, and the checker is
left asking where `render` comes from. A capability is supplied *for* a type, by
an [[hql/extensions|extension]] registering `render(Graph) -> Html`, not
inherited by it. HQL knows the type; the extension knows what can be done with
it. Whether a parameterized capability such as `Renderable[T]` is a type at all,
or only a registration the checker consults, is open — and it is the same
question the [trait direction](../../memory/trait-direction.md) records.

**Representation is not ancestry.** A Relation Card represents a `Relation`; it
is not one, and a `Relation` is not a `Card`. See below.

**Projection is not ancestry.** `knowledge | graph` yields a `Graph`, and
`Knowledge` is not thereby a supertype of anything. A projection is a function
— see [[graph-type]].

## `[T]` abstracts

A generic parameter is an unknown type that a declaration is written against,
so that one declaration serves a whole family:

```hql
type Edge[S, T] {
    source: S
    target: T
    data:   Data
}

type Graph[N, E]
```

`S` and `T` are not types with values of their own. They are positions, filled
at each use — `Edge[Card, Card]`, `Edge[Card, Concept]` — and the declaration is
checked once for all of them. This is what lets a graph over documents and a
graph over concepts share one definition without a node union or a `kind` field.

Functions abstract the same way:

```hql
map[A, B](f: A -> B) : Collection[A] -> Collection[B]
```

Nothing here is inferred away by the reader: `map` has one body, and `A` and `B`
are chosen by the call site. Users are not expected to write those parameters at
a call — they are inferred — but they exist in the signature.

> These pages write generics in square brackets, `Set[T]`, while
> [[hql/collections]] and [[hql/core]] write `Set<T>`. The two spellings are
> tracked as one unsettled choice and this card does not settle it; it uses
> square brackets so that `Edge[S, T]` here matches [[graph-type]].

## Where the two cooperate: bounds

A bare parameter admits every type, which is usually more freedom than a function
wants. A **bound** restricts it with the subtype relation:

```hql
fn titles[T](docs: Collection[T]) : Collection[String]
where T <: Doc
```

`T` is still unknown, and the body may now use everything `Doc` guarantees. The
call site supplies `Card`, `Person` or `Doc` itself, and each is checked against
the bound rather than against the body.

This is the whole of the interaction, and it is worth stating what it is not.
Subtyping describes relationships between concrete type domains that exist
whether or not any function is written. Generics let one function range over a
family of such domains. A bound is where a function says which part of the
lattice it needs, and `<:` is the only vocabulary it needs to say so:

```hql
fn show[T, R](x: T) : R
where T <: Renderable[R]
```

A separate constraint syntax — Rust's `T: Renderable[R]` — would introduce a
second relation symbol meaning something close enough to `<:` to be confusing and
different enough to have to be learned. One symbol is the stronger design, and
the more compact `fn show[T <: Renderable[R], R](x: T)` is the same relation
inlined. Which form the grammar takes is open; that it is `<:` in both is not.

**Variance is open.** `Card <: Doc` does not by itself decide whether
`Graph[Card, Link] <: Graph[Doc, Edge]` holds, and a function written over
`Graph[Doc, Edge]` is useless if it does not. Covariance is defensible while
graphs are immutable values and unsound as soon as one can be extended in place,
so the answer depends on decisions about construction that have not been made —
see [[graph-type]].

## Refinement establishes

Subtyping and generics are statements about types. Refinement is a statement
about a **value**: its structure becomes progressively better known, and the
value itself is never touched.

The starting point is [[data-type|Data]], which is not `Any`. `Data` already
says something strong — *this is a valid structured tree* — and everything that
follows says stronger things about regions of that one tree.

```text
authored YAML
  ↓
Data
  ↓  HMD knows some paths: tags, use, import, nav
Data refined by HMD
  ↓  extensions contribute schemas
Data refined by HMD and extension schemas
  ↓  the program tests existence and shape
locally refined further still
```

HMD's own specification reserves a small number of frontmatter keys and leaves
every other key to the author while still requiring it to reach consumers, which
is precisely a tree with a few known paths — see the
[language specification](https://hypermarkdown.org/wiki/hmd-lang-spec/) and the
[document model](https://hypermarkdown.org/proposals/HMD-0020/).

An extension refines by declaring what an existing region means:

```hql
import knowledge

card.metadata          : Data              // before
card.metadata          : KnowledgeMetadata // after
card.metadata.concepts : List[Concept]
```

The runtime value has not changed. What changed is what the checker can prove
about it, which is the single most important property of the mechanism: an
extension owns **type knowledge about a tree**, never the tree. Nothing is
converted, copied or wrapped, so a header does not become a record on the way in
and back again on the way out.

The same happens one layer down, on an ordinary authored header:

```yaml
database:
  engine: postgres
  host: db.example.org
```

```hql
card.header.database : Data?               // before
card.header.database : PostgresConfig      // once the shape is established
```

The subtree never declared itself to be a `PostgresConfig`. It became usable as
one because the required structure is there, and that is the intuition behind
duck typing — *if it has the shape, use it accordingly*. HQL is stricter than
that in one decisive respect:

```text
duck typing
    it looks like a Duck, so attempt the operation

HQL
    the known structure proves this value satisfies Duck,
    and operations are checked against the proof
```

So the accurate description of the mechanism is **open structural data with
progressive type refinement**: `Data` values keep their original tree
representation while HMD semantics, extensions, schemas and predicates
progressively establish stronger types for parts of that tree. The informal
gloss — rigorous duck typing — is fair, and the technical name is the one to
design against.

Two rules keep it honest.

**Refinement never converts.** The refined value is the same value; no `as`
rewrites it, and losing the refinement loses only knowledge, never data.

**Refinement must be checked.** A `type: WorksOn` entry in a header is an
author's claim, and `typed Relation` has to verify the contract. An unchecked
narrowing would let any authored key mint a type, which is the same rule that
stops `relation: parent` on an edge from making it a `Parent` — see
[[card-type]] and [[graph-type]]. What a failed narrowing yields is open.

## Relation is not a Card

`Relation[S, T] <: Card` appeared in several of these cards and is **never
true**. It is worth keeping the correction here, because the reason it is wrong
is exactly the boundary this card draws.

`Relation` is a first-class type in the knowledge vocabulary, beside `Concept`,
`Proposition` and `Evidence` — a meaningful relationship with typed endpoints,
its own identity, and evidence and provenance behind it. A relation acquires
none of that from a document. It can be imported from another namespace, derived
by a rule, or read out of a database, and in each case there is a relation and
no card anywhere.

A **Relation Card** is then what its name says: a card that *represents* a
relation, giving it a body, a name in the vault and incoming links of its own.

```hql
card.metadata.relation : Relation[S, T]
```

That is refinement, not narrowing. The card is a `Card`; the relation is a
`Relation`; neither is a specialization of the other, and the relation is
reached through the card's knowledge layer like any other metadata. Preferring a
Relation Card for a significant relationship — over an anonymous annotated edge
— is unchanged by this, and is argued in [[hql/knowledge]].

What `Relation` does narrow is the edge:

```hql
type Relation[S, T] <: Edge[S, T]
```

An edge has a source, a target and data; a relation has all three and adds
identity and evidence, so the narrowing is ordinary. A relation's identity is
its own, which is what makes the knowledge graph a multigraph able to retain two
conflicting claims about one pair — see [[graph-type]].

> This replaces the reading on [[card-type]], [[graph-type]] and the
> [knowledge model](../../models/domain/knowledge.md) that a Relation Card is a
> narrowing of `Card`, and the supertype-set form `Relation[S, T] <: {Card,
> Edge[S, T]}` that followed from it. The supertype-set syntax itself stands; it
> simply is not motivated by this case.

## The three at once

A card carrying a relation exercises all three mechanisms, each doing only its
own job:

```hql
type Card <: Doc                       // subtyping: a card is a document
type Relation[S, T] <: Edge[S, T]      // subtyping over a generic parent
card.metadata.relation : Relation[S, T] // refinement: this card carries one
```

The first says where `Card` sits for every card there will ever be. The second
does the same for relations, once, for every endpoint pairing. The third says
what has been established about one value, and could fail for the next card
along without disturbing either declaration.

## Open questions

- Whether narrowing is nominal or structural, and what a failed narrowing yields
  — an `Option`, a diagnostic, or a value carrying its failure.
- The spelling of validation and refinement at the call site: `validate[Person](card)`,
  a `where` clause, or a predicate that the checker records.
- Variance of generic parameters, which depends on whether constructed values
  are immutable.
- Square versus angle brackets for generics, unsettled across these cards.
- Whether a supertype set is genuinely an intersection type or only a list of
  declared parents, which decides what `X <: {A, B}` means when `A` and `B`
  conflict.
- Whether capability contracts such as `Renderable[T]` are types participating
  in `<:`, or registrations outside the type graph — and how either reading
  coexists with the preference against exposing trait machinery to users.
- How much refinement survives a boundary: a value passed to an extension, a
  cell, or a host and back.

See [[data-type]] for the tree being refined, [[card-type]] for the narrowing
from `Doc` and the card's two layers, [[graph-type]] for `Edge`, `Graph` and
variance, [[hql/knowledge]] for relations as knowledge, [[hql/collections]] for
the generic collections and [[hql/design-status]] for what remains undecided.
